\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Chirality and the volume element}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{Where the product of the represented generators is the volume
element, that product is #{\psI}. In even dimension #{n=2m}
the Euclidean identities are #{P^{\dagger}=(-1)^m P} and
#{P^2=(-1)^m\bbI} (\ref{fdip-009X}). The phase #{C=\ui^m P}
is the one for which #{C^{\dagger}=C} and #{C^2=\bbI}. The
volume element anticommutes with the generators, so the phase
remains a scalar. A grading that is not the volume element is
#{(-1)^{\deg}}. Mathematical physics writes
#{C=i^m\Gamma_1\cdots\Gamma_{2m}}, or #{\gamma_5} up to a phase.
Geometric calculus writes the pseudoscalar.}
